\name{sdrf_lint}
\alias{sdrf_lint}
\title{Find the concepts a set of SDRF documents annotated inconsistently}
\usage{
sdrf_lint(documents, timeout = 60)
}
\arguments{
\item{documents}{A character vector of paths. \strong{Name it} to choose each document's label, as
  for \code{\link{sdrf_pool}}: \code{c(cohort = "a.sdrf.tsv", partner = "b.sdrf.tsv")}. Name every element or
  none; mzLib's default label depends on where the files sit.}

\item{timeout}{Seconds to allow, or \code{NULL} to wait indefinitely.
}

}
\description{
Find the concepts a set of SDRF documents wrote inconsistently, one row per finding and spelling.

Validity is a property of one file; comparability is a property of the relationship between
files. Every document can pass \code{\link{sdrf_validate}} and the pooled table still be unusable because
one writes \code{"Homo sapiens"} and another \code{"homo sapiens"}. This is mzLib's \code{SdrfDriftLint}.
}
\details{
Columns unique per row by construction are never compared - \code{source name}, \code{assay name},
\code{comment[data file]}, \code{comment[searched data file]}, \code{comment[file uri]} and
\code{comment[source document]} - so differing file names are not drift. Reserved words and empty
cells are skipped too, so documents that say nothing lint clean: use \code{\link{sdrf_assess}} for that.
}
\value{
An \code{mzlibr_sdrf_drift}: \code{document_count} documents linted, \code{paths}, \code{labels},
  \code{finding_count} distinct findings (not rows), \code{caveats}, \code{column_names}, and \code{records}, a
  long data.frame with \strong{one row per finding x variant}: \code{finding_index} (1-based, most
  impactful first), \code{kind}, \code{concept}, \code{column_name} (\code{NA} for a \code{ColumnNameVariant}, which is
  about a name rather than a column's values), \code{variant_rank} (1 for the majority spelling),
  \code{value} (this spelling, as written), \code{occurrences} (how many documents used it), and
  \code{documents}, a list column of the labels of those documents.

}
\section{Kinds of drift}{


\code{AccessionNameConflict} - one accession written with several names. \code{NameAccessionConflict} -
one name with several accessions, the serious one. \code{MixedTermAndFreeText} - a column mixing
controlled-vocabulary terms and free text. \code{ColumnNameVariant} - column names differing only by
case or spacing. \code{ValueCaseVariant} - values differing only by case. The majority spelling is
not advice: it is only the most common.

}
